  "output": "Bug reproduced. Here's the full picture — including an important nuance about *when* it happens.\n\n## Reproduction\n\n**Input file (`sample.csv`):**\n```\nname,city,note\n\"Doe, John\", \"Paris, France\", frequent traveler\n```\n(Note: there's a space after each comma before the opening quote.)\n\n**Incorrect output:**\n```\nTotal rows: 1\nTotal columns: 3\nFirst data row: ['Doe, John', ' \"Paris', ' France\"', ' frequent traveler']\n```\n\nThe tool reports **4 fields instead of 3**, and the `' \"Paris'` / `' France\"'` fragments show the quoted field was split in half. The stray quote characters leaking into the output are the giveaway.\n\n## The nuance: it's not the quoted comma itself\n\nI ran a control with the same data using strict RFC-4180 quoting (no space between the delimiter and the opening quote):\n\n**`sample_ok.csv`:**\n```\n\"Doe, John\",\"Paris, France\",frequent traveler\n```\n\n**Output — correct:**\n```\nTotal rows: 1\nTotal columns: 3\nFirst data row: ['Doe, John', 'Paris, France', 'frequent traveler']\n```\n\nSo the tool already handles quoted commas fine; the failure mode is specifically **whitespace between the comma and the opening quote** (``, \"Paris, France\"``). That's an extremely common pattern from hand-edited files and certain exporters (some database and spreadsheet exports emit `` `\", \" `` as the separator), so the user's report is legitimate for that class of input — the current code uses `csv.reader` defaults, which keep `skipinitialspace=False`.\n\nA final probe confirmed the mechanism — same line, both parser modes:\n\n```python\ncsv.reader([line])                      # ['Doe, John', ' \"Paris', ' France\"', ' frequent traveler']\ncsv.reader([line], skipinitialspace=True)  # ['Doe, John', 'Paris, France', 'frequent traveler']\n```\n\n## Suggested fix (one line)\n\nPass `skipinitialspace=True` to `csv.reader` in `parse_csv()`. It fixes the reported case, and it doesn't alter the already-correct strict-quoting behavior or the preserved-spaces-inside-quotes behavior (the existing test `test_parse_csv_preserves_spaces_in_quotes` covers that distinction — spaces *inside* quotes stay intact either way).\n\nSay the word and I'll apply the fix and re-run the test suite, adding a regression test from `sample.csv`.",
